@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Plan-and-Solve (zero-shot: devise a plan, then carry it out)
' Paper: Plan-and-Solve Prompting: Improving Zero-Shot Chain-of-Thought Reasoning by Large Language Models
' URL:   https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.04091
' Level: 3
' Formerly: 3-meta-prompt-loops/plan_and_solve.puml (kebab-case rename). The earlier diagram drew a
'           Planner agent, an Executor agent and a Tool Set with an execution loop — an L4 harness
'           shape. The cited paper is prompt-only: no tools, no agents, no weight change. Redrawn to
'           match its citation; the tool-executing variant belongs at L4 if it is wanted.

actor User
participant "LLM (single model)" as LLM <<app>>

User ARROW_MAIN LLM : task + trigger prompt\n'let's first understand the problem\nand devise a plan'
activate LLM

LLM ARROW_MAIN LLM : **Phase 1 — plan**\nextract variables, state the sub-tasks
note right of LLM
  Still one completion. The "plan" is
  generated //text//, not a data structure
  handed to another component.
end note

LLM ARROW_MAIN LLM : **Phase 2 — solve**\ncarry out the sub-tasks in order,\nshowing intermediate results

LLM ARROW_MAIN User : final answer
deactivate LLM

note over LLM
  **Why this is Level 3 and not Level 4.** The whole
  method is a better //trigger prompt// than "let's
  think step by step": it addresses zero-shot CoT's
  missing-step and calculation errors by forcing an
  explicit plan first. Weights are frozen, and
  nothing outside the model is called — no tools, no
  retrieval, no orchestrator. That is exactly the
  L3/L4 boundary.
  ----
  Contrast ""cot.puml"" (no planning phase) and
  ""react.puml"" (L3 reasoning that //does// interleave
  tool calls, which is why ReAct straddles the line).
end note
@enduml
